{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.4,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U1", "J5", "C2"],
      "pins": ["U1.2", "U1.3", "J5.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On the regulator sheet, U1 (LM1117-5.0) has its input pin VI (U1.2) tied to the +5V net (the same net that C2 decouples and that the VO pin U1.3 is also tied to). The raw supply net VCC (from J5.2 'PWR') is not connected to U1.2; it only reaches +5V through the VCC_STABLE jumper JP1.",
      "expected": "For an LM1117-5.0 used to generate the +5V rail, VIN (U1.2) must be connected to the unregulated input rail (VCC from J5), and VOUT (U1.3) to the regulated +5V net. VIN and VOUT must not be on the same net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 LM1117-5.0: pin 2 'VI' wire runs to '+5V' (with C2 100nF); pin 3 'VO' wire also runs to '+5V'"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J5 PWR pin 2 -> VCC (separate net from +5V, joined only via JP1 VCC_STABLE)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V regulator cannot regulate the raw VCC supply: with VIN shorted to VOUT the part does nothing, so the +5 V rail (which powers both ATtiny MCUs) exists only if the VCC_STABLE jumper JP1 is closed, directly shorting VCC to +5 V and bypassing the regulator entirely (and placing the input decoupling cap C2 on the wrong side).",
      "confidence": 0.4
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R1"],
      "pins": ["R1", "Q1.2", "Q1.3"],
      "nets": ["VCC", "VCC_OUT"],
      "observed": "R1 is drawn with value '10' (10 ohm) connecting VCC to the VCC_OUT node (the drain of the high-side PMOS Q1), i.e. in parallel with the Q1 channel.",
      "expected": "A resistor bridging the switched supply to the fan rail in this position should be a high-value gate/bleed resistor (or be omitted); a 10-ohm parallel path defeats the high-side switch.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R1 value '10' between VCC and the Q1/Q2/VCC_OUT node, while every other resistor is 1K/10K"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 10-ohm path from VCC to VCC_OUT keeps the fan partially powered when Q1 is off (fan cannot fully stop) and, if Q2 is driven, sinks ~0.5 A through R1/Q2.",
      "confidence": 0.2
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing supports a miswired 5 V regulator: U1's VIN pin is tied to the +5 V output net instead of the raw VCC input, so the regulator cannot generate +5 V and the rail only appears by shorting VCC to +5 V through the VCC_STABLE jumper. A secondary, lower-confidence concern is R1 = 10 ohm bridging the high-side switch."
}

